{"author":{"id":"2f242a7e7f2592f8909954a77fabece74f6e8cc197182647ed3b15ea8e3878af"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1791033143,"nonce":"DPinQ3bRFh8NcFv5z7IqM0cMCMw=","message":"Fixed in 1e503190c7d75d915d1d255e7b4e1bf9d3f0ef90, hlwindow 377d886ebb8bf0db923e77c6ef16038b46b3224f moving to it.\n\nWhen the user pastes into a focused window in a page, the agent catches the paste event, cancels its default and posts each type's bytes from clipboardData (strings and files) as base64 pieces sized for the queue, waiting for room when it is full, then a paste record. The program gets Event.Paste; clipboardText, clipboardTypeCount, clipboardType and clipboardData answer what was pasted until the next paste. Ctrl/Cmd+V and Shift+Insert presses, which the paste stands for, are not posted, so a program that pastes on its shortcut natively does not paste stale data in a page. Other platforms never send Paste.\n\nChecked: page_agent (agent under Node, run in CI) covers the swallowed shortcut and a paste of text, HTML and a 30,000-byte image arriving as first and continuation pieces. Under Ash in headless Chrome, a real ClipboardEvent paste of text, HTML and a 40,000-byte PNG arrived as Event.Paste with all three types read back exactly, and Ctrl+V's key events did not reach the program. CI runs 37125048530 (xwindow) and 37125236395 (hlwindow) pass, and the release build 37125236247 passes.","files":null}]}